✅ QMD v5.0 — Complete Integration Package
Everything required to run the QMD v5.0 kernel (C/C++ environments, ESC firmware, embedded systems).

1. Fixed-Point Representation (Mandatory)
QMD v5.0 uses Q12.20 fixed-point:
    • 12 integer bits
    • 20 fractional bits
    • wide enough to hold η, τ, and all polynomial intermediate values
    • multiplications shift >>20
Engineers need:
typedef int32_t q20_t;
#define Q20_ONE (1 << 20)
#define FLOAT_TO_Q20(x)   ((q20_t)((x) * (float)(1<<20)))
#define Q20_TO_FLOAT(x)   ((float)(x) / (float)(1<<20))
This is all that is required for fixed-point math.

2. QMD v5.0 Coefficients (Final)
static const q20_t C0_fp =  575821;
static const q20_t C2_fp =  596139;
static const q20_t C4_fp = -253691;
These are locked and final.
These came from the global optimum sweep and they should not be changed.

3. Core Polynomial Kernel (η)
Input:
tau_fp — Q12.20
Output:
eta_fp — Q12.20
static inline q20_t qmd5_eta(q20_t tau_fp)
{
    q20_t dx  = tau_fp - Q20_ONE;        // (tau – 1)
    q20_t dx2 = (int64_t)dx*dx >> 20;
    q20_t dx4 = (int64_t)dx2*dx2 >> 20;

    return C0_fp
         + (int64_t)C2_fp * dx2
         + (int64_t)C4_fp * dx4;
}
This contains:
    • no floats
    • no branches
    • no lookup tables
    • constant-time performance

4. Analytic Derivative Kernel (η′)
Input:
tau_fp — Q12.20
Output:
eta_prime_fp — Q12.20
static inline q20_t qmd5_eta_prime(q20_t tau_fp)
{
    q20_t dx = tau_fp - Q20_ONE;

    // dx³ in Q12.20: dx*dx*dx >> 40
    q20_t dx3 = (q20_t)(((int64_t)dx * dx >> 20) * dx >> 20);

    q20_t term1 = ((int64_t)(2 * C2_fp) * dx)  >> 20;
    q20_t term3 = ((int64_t)(4 * C4_fp) * dx3) >> 20;

    return term1 + term3;
}
This is the core of QMD v5.0’s stability and efficiency gains.

5. Peak Point (Single Value)
QMD v5.0’s polynomial is symmetric around τ = 1.0, therefore:
#define QMD5_TAU_PEAK_FP  (1 << 20)
Engineering consequence:
    • No root-finding
    • No Newton loops
    • No iteration count variance
    • No numeric derivative required
The control loop now runs deterministically.

6. Runtime Usage Pattern
A typical ESC modulation loop:
q20_t tau_fp = compute_tau_somehow(); // user’s observer

q20_t eta_fp  = qmd5_eta(tau_fp);
q20_t etap_fp = qmd5_eta_prime(tau_fp);

// Use η and η′ however your modulation model requires
That’s it.
No lookups.
No numeric differentiation.
No post-processing.

7. Required Constraints (What Engineers Must Know)
7.1 Allowed τ Range
The polynomial is guaranteed stable in:
0.50 ≤ τ ≤ 1.50
If engineers provide values outside this, they should clamp:
if (tau_fp < FLOAT_TO_Q20(0.5f)) tau_fp = FLOAT_TO_Q20(0.5f);
if (tau_fp > FLOAT_TO_Q20(1.5f)) tau_fp = FLOAT_TO_Q20(1.5f);
7.2 Overflow Safety
All intermediate products use int64_t.
This is mandatory.

8. Required Utility (τ Conversion)
If ESC engineers measure τ in float:
float tau       = measured_tau;
q20_t tau_fp    = FLOAT_TO_Q20(tau);
q20_t eta_fp    = qmd5_eta(tau_fp);
float eta       = Q20_TO_FLOAT(eta_fp);
This ensures cross-platform reproducibility.

9. Optional Diagnostic / Testing Code
(Not needed in production, but used in development.)
Sweep test:
for (int i = 0; i <= 100; i++)
{
    float tau = 0.50f + 1.00f * (i / 100.0f);
    q20_t tau_fp = FLOAT_TO_Q20(tau);

    q20_t e  = qmd5_eta(tau_fp);
    q20_t ep = qmd5_eta_prime(tau_fp);

    printf("%f, %f, %f\n",
        tau,
        Q20_TO_FLOAT(e),
        Q20_TO_FLOAT(ep));
}

